{"name":"River Clay","hex":"#c7b5a9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"river","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large and often winding stream which drains a land mass, carrying water down from higher areas to a lower point, oftentimes ending in another body of water, such as an ocean or in an inland sea.","Any large flow of a liquid in a single body."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve one’s hand to beat another player on the final card in a poker game."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river"},{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"}],"entries":[{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River","wikidata":"Q4022"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay","wikidata":"Q42302"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png/500px-NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png/500px-NorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The major drainage basins in North America","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANorthAmerica-WaterDivides.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/500px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/1280px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg/500px-Perito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Melting toe of the Perito Moreno Glacier in Los Glaciares National Park, Argentina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APerito_Moreno_Glacier_2023.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/